//  PETPORTS -- PET TREAT FLAVORS
//
//  What a reagent does when the player drops it into the upcycler's reagent
//  slot, and how long it lasts.
//
//  A PET TREAT IS THE ONLY FUEL. Units do not eat crafting materials or produce
//  directly -- everything goes through the upcycler, which is what makes the
//  machine load bearing rather than optional. Flavor is the whole of the
//  preference system: a unit rolls a preferred FLAVOR, and eating it refills
//  more of the fuel bar than eating anything else.
//
//  A WRONG FLAVOR IS EXACTLY AS GOOD AS NO FLAVOR, never worse. That is what
//  keeps the system opt-in: a player who ignores reagents entirely loses a
//  bonus and is never punished, and one who flavors the wrong thing has
//  wasted a reagent rather than ruined a batch.
//
//  FLAVORING COSTS ENTROPY AND THAT IS THE POINT. The upcycler exists to
//  collapse a hundred junk item types into one, and flavors spend some of that
//  back -- seven treat types instead of one. So a player stockpiles only the
//  flavors their fleet's rolled preferences justify, and the rest stay fodder.
//  Seven is the ceiling of that cost and it is one restock crate wide.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WEIGHT IS HOW MANY TREATS ONE REAGENT FLAVORS
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  There is no second number and no efficiency stat: the batch length IS the
//  value. A reagent worth 8 flavors a full run; one worth 1 flavors a single
//  treat, so using it in bulk means feeding the slot in bulk.
//
//  WEIGHTS ARE AUTHORED, NOT DERIVED, and price is why. Price is the axis the
//  upcycler already values its INPUT on, and it fails completely here: Snowflake,
//  Glow Fibre, Plant Fibre, Toxic Waste and every petal are all price 0, and
//  those are exactly the abundant items the weight exists to price down.
//
//      8   The augment materials. Scorched Core, Cryonic Extract, Venom Sample,
//          Static Cell, Living Root and Phase Matter share ONE acquisition path
//          and one price -- treasure pools plus a handful of recipes, 50px
//          each. Six flavors therefore have anchors that cost the same to
//          obtain, so no flavor is cheaper to supply than another by
//          construction rather than by tuning. Savory has no member here on
//          purpose: meat is trivially available and an 8 would make it the
//          cheapest flavor in the game.
//
//      4   Deliberate acquisition. A monster part, or something cooked.
//
//      2   Farmed or gathered on purpose. Most produce.
//
//      1   Incidental. Picked up while doing something else, in quantities
//          nobody counts -- Snowflake, Bone, Plant Fibre, Metal Coated Wood.
//          These belong in their flavor on taste and would trivialise it at
//          any higher number.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  MATCHED BY NAME, WHICH IS NOT A PREFERENCE
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  The filter manifest sorts on categories and tags and neither works here. An
//  asset scan across every food, produce and crafting material in the game
//  found exactly ONE itemTag in existence -- "reagent", on 115 items, which is
//  most of them. There is nothing to discriminate on, so every entry is a name.
//
//  FAIL CLOSED. A reagent not named here is refused by the slot rather than
//  silently producing unflavored treats. An unlisted item that quietly did
//  nothing would read as a bug in the machine.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  `color` IS THE BLIP COLOUR, AND IT LIVES HERE ON PURPOSE
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  The reagent slot's charge display fills one blip per treat a reagent has
//  bought, tinted so the player can see WHICH flavour is queued without reading
//  anything. Six digits of RRGGBB, fed to "?multiply=" against a white sprite
//  with a black outline -- black is untouched by a multiply, white becomes the
//  tint exactly. Same trick the crosshairs use.
//
//  IT BELONGS TO THE FLAVOUR, NOT TO THE PANE. A mod adding an eighth flavour
//  gets a blip colour by adding one field, rather than by us keeping a table of
//  colours somewhere that only knows about the seven we shipped.
//
//  THESE SEVEN ARE THE TREAT PALETTE and are not arbitrary: they are spread
//  across LIGHTNESS as well as hue so they survive red-green colour blindness,
//  where a naive palette collapses spicy into zesty and savory. See the note in
//  the .item files before changing one.
//
//  FOR MODDERS: patch /flavors/<flavor>/reagents/<itemName> with a weight.
//  Adding a whole flavor means adding the treat ITEM as well -- the entries
//  here name one, and a flavor whose item does not exist cannot be produced.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  EVERY NAME BELOW WAS CHECKED AGAINST AN UNPACKED VANILLA TREE
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  Vanilla is full of crafting materials that still have a definition file and
//  no way to obtain them: 28 of the 117 crafting materials are named by nothing
//  in the game -- not a treasure pool, not a monster, not a biome, not a quest,
//  not a recipe. Molten Core, Ice Crystal, Ancient Bones, Alien Wood Sap, Wild
//  Vines, the hardware drawer and most of the robot parts are all dead, and a
//  flavor class built on one would look correct and never fire. They were
//  found with workbench/tools/petports_fooddump.py and removed.
//
//  ONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION: bugshell. Nothing in vanilla hands it over, and it
//  is kept because a number of mods use it.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  PREPARED DISHES ARE DERIVED, NOT ASSIGNED
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//  The 134 prepared dishes and 28 drinks were not sorted by hand. Each one takes
//  the flavor of its own INGREDIENTS, read out of its recipe, so a dish's flavor
//  is a consequence of the reagent table above rather than a second set of
//  judgements that can drift away from it. Correcting one produce entry corrects
//  every dish downstream of it.
//
//  Resolved with workbench/tools/petports_fooddump.py's `from` column, which
//  lists every input of every recipe that makes an item. Intermediate dishes
//  recurse: Toxic Jam Donut is made from toxictopjam, which is made from
//  toxictop, which is sour.
//
//  THREE RULES, AND TWO OF THEM WERE FORCED BY THE DATA:
//
//  1. STAPLES DO NOT VOTE. Sugar and Wheat are each in 41 dishes, Egg in 21,
//     Rice and Cheese in 11. They are structure, not seasoning -- sugar in a
//     curry does not make it sweet, and the first pass had Wheat making Apple
//     Crumble SAVORY. wheat, rice, egg, milk, cheese, sugar and bread are
//     excluded from the vote. They remain reagents in their own right; this
//     only stops them deciding somebody else's dish. A dish that is nothing but
//     staples falls back to letting them vote rather than going unflavored.
//
//  2. ASSERTIVE FLAVORS DOMINATE, in this order:
//
//         spicy > sour > savory > bitter > sharp > zesty > sweet
//
//     A chili makes anything spicy no matter what else is in the pot. Savory
//     sits above bitter and sharp because meat carries a dish: an omelette with
//     mushroom in it is an omelette, and a risotto with reefpod in it is a
//     risotto. An earlier ordering had bitter higher and turned both into
//     vegetable dishes.
//
//  3. EVERY DISH IS WEIGHT 8, the same as an augment material. Preparing food
//     is player effort and prepared food barely stacks because it decays, so a
//     dish is expensive per unit in a way a Scorched Core is not -- and a
//     reagent that rots in the crate is self-limiting without a rule saying so.
//
//  HAND ASSIGNMENTS MUST BE SEEDED BEFORE DERIVING, NOT MERGED AFTER, and
//  getting that backwards produced the one wrong answer in the set. Coconut
//  Rice is [coconutmilk, rice]; rice is a staple and does not vote, and when it
//  was resolved Coconut Drink had not been assigned yet -- so the recursion went
//  into `coconut`, found nothing, and fell through to the all-staples fallback
//  where rice made it SAVORY. Only a dish downstream of a hand-assigned item can
//  hit this, and only one was.
//
//  SIX ITEMS HAVE NO USABLE RECIPE and are assigned by hand: Oculemonade sour,
//  Reef-Cola bitter, Soda, Coconut Drink and Milk sweet, Pizza savory. Bottled
//  Water is deliberately absent -- water has no flavor and stays ordinary
//  fodder.
//
//  MILK IS SWEET ONLY BECAUSE SWEET IS THE BOTTOM OF THE DOMINANCE ORDER. It
//  was assigned savory first and had to be pulled: milk is a STAPLE, and a
//  staple that is also a reagent votes in the all-staples fallback, where savory
//  outranks sweet and turned Coconut Ice Cream savory. Sweet cannot do that --
//  it loses every tie it enters, so a staple carrying it can never win somebody
//  else's dish. Verified: putting it back as sweet changed no dish at all.
//  Sweet is the only safe flavor for a staple to hold.
//
//  It sits at 2 rather than 8 despite being hand-assigned: the hand assignments
//  decide which FLAVOR an item has, never what it is WORTH, and milk is bought
//  by the crate at the outpost. Weight follows acquisition, always.
//
//  TWO RECIPE INPUTS ARE INVISIBLE TO THE SCAN and are assigned by hand for the
//  same reason: `coconut` and `snowball`, both sweet. Neither is in the report,
//  because fooddump's extension list covers items and objects and not thrown
//  items -- so a recipe can name an ingredient the report cannot see, and the
//  derivation reads that as "no flavor" rather than flagging it. Only these two
//  across all 160 dishes, but check for more before trusting a future rerun.
//
//  SPOT-CHECKED AGAINST THE WIKI'S FOOD PAGE, 217 entries. Every edible item in
//  the assets is mapped except Bottled Water. Four the wiki lists -- Flesh Lump,
//  Cooked Flesh Lump, Meat Chunks and Offal -- do not exist in the unpacked tree
//  at all and were not added.
//
//  SIX MAPPED ITEMS ARE UNOBTAINABLE IN VANILLA AND STAY MAPPED. Orange is the
//  clearest: its 313 references are the COLOR word, not the fruit -- the exact
//  false positive the reference test warns about -- and nothing drops it. Green
//  Apple, Pizza and Raw Ham are named by `interface` and nothing else, Cooked
//  Ham by a quest, Box of Chocolates is seasonal. They are kept because mods
//  routinely ship these as expected produce, and an entry for something nobody
//  can obtain costs nothing while a missing one has to be noticed and patched.
//  Same reasoning as bugshell.

